<p>Health tech projects often stall in the same place: engineering is ready, but data access agreements, ethics approvals and security reviews take months. Synthetic data fills the gap. Used well, it lets a team build and test an entire pipeline before seeing a single real record. Used badly, it produces a model that looks great in testing and fails on contact with a real hospital.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>What Synthea is</h2>
<p>Synthea is an open-source synthetic patient generator, described in a 2018 JAMIA paper. It simulates patient lifespans using publicly available data, covering common reasons for primary care visits and high-burden chronic conditions in the United States, and outputs records in formats such as HL7 FHIR and C-CDA, free of legal and privacy restrictions<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. For FHIR and OMOP pipeline work, it is the default starting point.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The realism gap</h2>
<p>Synthetic data is clean in ways real data never is. A 2026 study using Synthea output notes that real EHR records contain transcription errors, inconsistent formatting, missing fields, local coding conventions and data quality issues not present in Synthea data<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. An evaluation presented at the OHDSI symposium summarised earlier findings that some Synthea distributions needed external modification to be realistic, and that more validation was needed for chronic disease tracking<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. And it is American by design: teams in other countries have had to adapt it to local profiles<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Synthetic data tells you whether your system works. It cannot tell you whether your model is right.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Synthetic, de-identified or real: which for what</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Synthetic</th><th>De-identified</th><th>Real (controlled)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Building FHIR and OMOP pipelines</td><td>Ideal</td><td>Useful for edge cases</td><td>Final validation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Developer environments and demos</td><td>Ideal</td><td>Rarely needed</td><td>Avoid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Load and performance testing</td><td>Ideal, at any scale</td><td>Not needed</td><td>Avoid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data quality rules</td><td>Too clean to test them</td><td>Good</td><td>Best</td></tr>
<tr><td>Model training</td><td>Prototyping only</td><td>Often appropriate</td><td>When governance allows</td></tr>
<tr><td>Clinical performance and bias evaluation</td><td>Not valid</td><td>Possible with care</td><td>Required</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>De-identified data sits in the middle. HHS guidance describes two HIPAA methods: Safe Harbor, removing specified identifiers, and Expert Determination, where a qualified expert concludes the risk of re-identification is very small<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Free-text notes are the hard part; identifiers hide in narrative.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>How I use synthetic data in projects</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Build the whole pipeline on synthetic data first:</strong> ingestion, <a href="/insights/fhir-to-omop-architecture/">FHIR to OMOP</a> mapping, APIs and dashboards. Access requests run in parallel.</li>
<li><strong>Inject realistic mess deliberately.</strong> Add missing fields, duplicate patients, odd codes and late-arriving data, so the pipeline's error handling is exercised before real data arrives.</li>
<li><strong>Keep developer environments synthetic permanently.</strong> Real data in development is one of the commonest sources of PHI exposure, which matters under <a href="/insights/hipaa-security-rule-proposal-ai-platforms/">HIPAA security expectations</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Validate on real data under governance.</strong> Run <a href="/insights/omop-data-quality-checks/">data quality checks</a> and model evaluations in a controlled environment before any claim about performance.</li>
<li><strong>Label results honestly.</strong> Any metric computed on synthetic data is marked as such in reports, so nobody mistakes a pipeline test for clinical evidence.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>For AI and LLM work</h2>
<p>Synthetic records are excellent for testing LLM applications end to end: prompt construction from FHIR resources, retrieval, safety filters and integration, as in <a href="/insights/ambient-ai-scribes-architecture/">ambient AI scribes</a>. They are poor evidence of how a model will handle real notes and real patients. Build your <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation sets</a> from de-identified or governed real data wherever the claim is about clinical quality.</p>
</section>

